+# Best AI Tools for Tax Professionals 2026
+
+[![Awesome](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/sindresorhus/awesome@main/media/badge.svg)](https://github.com/sindresorhus/awesome)
+[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
+![Topics](https://img.shields.io/github/stars/GagnDeep/awesome-best-ai-tools-for-tax-professionals-2026?style=social)
+
+> This curated list of the best AI tools for tax professionals in 2026 focuses on open source projects that exist as public GitHub repositories and can be self-hosted, audited, and adapted for real tax workflows. Because the GitHub ecosystem still has very few tax-specific AI repos, this list concentrates on the building blocks tax teams actually use: OCR, PDF extraction, private research, local LLMs, and workflow automation.
+
+## Table of Contents
+- [TL;DR](#tldr)
+- [Why This List](#why-this-list)
+- [Open Source Tools](#open-source-tools)
+- [FAQ](#faq)
+- [Contributing](#contributing)
+- [License](#license)
+
+## TL;DR
+- `Docling`, `Unstructured`, `OCRmyPDF`, and `Surya` are strong starting points for scanned returns, notices, workpapers, and source documents.
+- `AnythingLLM`, `Open WebUI`, `LangChain`, `LlamaIndex`, and `Haystack` are useful for private tax research assistants over internal files.
+- `Ollama`, `llama.cpp`, `vLLM`, `LocalAI`, and `Jan` cover local model execution when client confidentiality matters.
+- `n8n`, `Activepieces`, `Flowise`, `Langflow`, and `CrewAI` help turn extraction and research into repeatable internal workflows.
+- Honest gap: there are still fewer than 5 strong tax-specific open source AI copilots on GitHub, so most production stacks are assembled from adjacent document-AI and automation repos.
+
+## Why This List
+Tax teams handle sensitive PDFs, structured forms, invoices, notices, and research memos that often should not be pushed into closed SaaS black boxes. The best AI tools for tax professionals are therefore usually open source components that can be reviewed, self-hosted, and combined into secure internal workflows. This list only includes tools with public `github.com` repositories, and it notes a real market gap where tax-specific OSS AI remains thin.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Document AI, OCR, and PDF Extraction
+
+#### [OCRmyPDF](https://github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF)
+> **Description:** OCRmyPDF adds an OCR text layer to scanned PDFs so they become searchable and easier to pipe into downstream extraction, review, and retention workflows. Its README emphasizes preserving the original document while optimizing images, deskewing pages, and integrating with standard PDF pipelines. For tax professionals, that makes it a practical front door for scanned client packets, notices, and legacy workpapers before they hit a RAG or extraction system. GitHub showed a recent 2026 activity date, Python as the primary language, and an MPL-2.0 license.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF](https://github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF)
+- **Stars:** 24k ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MPL-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-03-30
+- **Category:** OCR, PDF, preprocessing
+- **Best for:** making scanned tax PDFs searchable before extraction
+
