+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for tax professionals in 2026?
+For most teams, the strongest starting stack is `Docling` or `Unstructured` for ingestion, `OCRmyPDF` or `Surya` for scanned files, `AnythingLLM` or `Open WebUI` for private document chat, and `Ollama` or `LocalAI` for local inference.
+
+### Are there any tax-specific open source AI copilots on GitHub?
+Very few. That is the main gap this repository documents honestly. Most serious tax teams still assemble their stack from document AI, retrieval, model serving, and workflow automation projects rather than adopting a pure tax-specific OSS copilot.
+
+### Which open source AI repos are best for scanned tax returns and notices?
+`OCRmyPDF`, `PaddleOCR`, `Surya`, `Docling`, and `LayoutParser` are the most relevant categories for scanned packets, notices, statements, and image-heavy source documents.
+
+### What is the best self-hosted AI setup for confidential tax documents?
+A practical pattern is `OCRmyPDF` or `Surya` for preprocessing, `Docling` or `Unstructured` for parsing, `AnythingLLM` or `RAGFlow` for retrieval, and `Ollama`, `LocalAI`, or `vLLM` for private model serving.
+
+### Which GitHub tools help with tax research memos and internal knowledge bases?
+`LangChain`, `LlamaIndex`, `Haystack`, `AnythingLLM`, and `RAGFlow` are the most useful for building retrieval-backed research assistants over internal templates, prior memos, and reference materials.
+
+### Which open source AI tools are best for tax workflow automation?
+`n8n`, `Activepieces`, `Flowise`, `Langflow`, and `CrewAI` are the strongest workflow-oriented picks in this list, depending on whether the need is operational automation, visual AI flows, or agent orchestration.
