+## TL;DR
+- `Docling`, `Unstructured`, `OCRmyPDF`, and `Surya` are strong starting points for scanned returns, notices, workpapers, and source documents.
+- `AnythingLLM`, `Open WebUI`, `LangChain`, `LlamaIndex`, and `Haystack` are useful for private tax research assistants over internal files.
+- `Ollama`, `llama.cpp`, `vLLM`, `LocalAI`, and `Jan` cover local model execution when client confidentiality matters.
+- `n8n`, `Activepieces`, `Flowise`, `Langflow`, and `CrewAI` help turn extraction and research into repeatable internal workflows.
+- Honest gap: there are still fewer than 5 strong tax-specific open source AI copilots on GitHub, so most production stacks are assembled from adjacent document-AI and automation repos.
+
+## Why This List
+Tax teams handle sensitive PDFs, structured forms, invoices, notices, and research memos that often should not be pushed into closed SaaS black boxes. The best AI tools for tax professionals are therefore usually open source components that can be reviewed, self-hosted, and combined into secure internal workflows. This list only includes tools with public `github.com` repositories, and it notes a real market gap where tax-specific OSS AI remains thin.
+
